In article <C3yL5H.AAz@ukelele.GCR.COM> kkrueg@ukelele.GCR.COM writes:
>This proposed multi-player hack (I suggest "MultHack") would indeed be
>complex.  Either the idea of a party of adventurers could be used, or
>separate questers, with a few changes:
>
>4) Timing's simple.  One cycle per second, no paralysis or sleep longer than
>   sixty seconds.  Other effects can be scaled.

NO. The way to do this is - keep the current system _except_ when players
interact. If a player attacks another (or does something else, e.g. #chat)
then that action _cannot_ proceed until the destination player takes some
action (moves, fights back, or anything else). The destination player is
notified of the action having taken place immediately, but the action only
takes effect when he/she next moves. I.e. attacks would be notified
as "Balkanaan attacks you!". Whether "Balkanaan hits!" or not would
only happen when the destination player makes a move.

Should the destination player be out to lunch, then the first player
will get fed up as he/she will be locked into an incomplete action.
To solve this, there should be a disengage key which would complete
a previously initiated action but without affecting the destination
player. Such inactive players could be visualised as transparent
ghost-like players partially in another dimension ("Your sword
slices through the ghost-like Balkanaan!").

Thus interplayer combat can occur fairly, with each player getting
one response per attack.

The scheme has to take account of fast/slow players (only the last action
in a multiple set is actually locked), and also of thrown and zapped
attacks ("The bolt lightning passes through the ghost-like Balkanaan!").

To avoid players fighting until near death then just refusing to respond,
hoping that the other player will give up and go away, once combat
has commenced and been responded to there should be a 10 second timeout
on all further interactions between those two players as long as they
remain on the same level. Book keeping for this would be fairly simple,
as only the last interplayer encounter would need to be remembered for
each player, and unless both players' most recent interplayer encounter
was with each other they would be lockstepped as normal.

In this system the game proceeds as normal most of the time, and
even when interacting with other players the system is still "turn"
based. Thus paralysis, etc. need not be changed.

Other monsters would need to be considered as players. They should
get a move every time any player on the same level moves. However
when they attack someone, then they should be lockstepped with that
person.

I once wrote a paper on how to implement this kind of system for MUDs;
there are some minor complications for group combat (multiple players
ganging up on a dragon, for instance). I can haul this out of the
archives if anyone is really interested...

